    "title": "LESSON 1: HOW SWAHILI SOUNDS",
    "header_color": "green",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 4,
        "content": [
          "Before the sounds, a page about the cards.",
          "",
          "A grey cue sits left of every headword.",
          "On a noun it is the word's class — its",
          "ngeli — as a prefix pair, one and many:",
          "",
          "   n/n    salama -> salama  (no change)",
          "   m/wa   mtoto  -> watoto  (people)",
          "   ji/ma  jambo  -> mambo",
          "",
          "Hear the pairs:                    Press 1",
          "",
          "Verbs carry no cue, and the voice never",
          "reads one — but it is part of what you",
          "memorise: the class runs the grammar.",
          "",
          "Lessons 3 and 7 open the engine; the rest",
          "arrive with their first card."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Salama. Salama. Mtoto. Watoto. Jambo. Mambo."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 4,
        "content": [
          "Karibu! Swahili is spelled exactly as it",
          "sounds. Five vowels, always pure, never",
          "silent:",
          "",
          "a = 'ah' as in father              Press 1",
          "   salama, sawa, hapana",
          "",
          "e = 'eh' as in bet                 Press 2",
          "   heri, kwaheri, mzee",
          "",
          "i = 'ee' as in machine             Press 3",
          "   rafiki, jina, bibi",
          "",
          "o = 'oh' as in note                Press 4",
          "   hodi, pole, ndiyo",
          "",
          "u = 'oo' as in flute               Press 5",
          "   bwana, salamu, starehe"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Salama. Sawa. Hapana.",
          "2": "Heri. Kwaheri. Mzee.",
          "3": "Rafiki. Jina. Bibi.",
          "4": "Hodi. Pole. Ndiyo.",
          "5": "Bwana. Salamu. Starehe."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 4,
        "content": [
          "A few consonants worth meeting early:",
          "",
          "dh = 'th' in this                  Press 1",
          "   tafadhali",
          "",
          "sh = as in English 'ship'          Press 2",
          "   shikamoo, kushukuru",
          "",
          "ny = 'ni' in onion                 Press 3",
          "   nyanya (grandmother — soon!)",
          "",
          "ng' = 'ng' in singer, no hard g    Press 4",
          "   ng'ombe (a cow — later!)",
          "",
          "j is soft, as in 'jam': jambo, jina.",
          "r is lightly rolled: rafiki, heri."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Tafadhali.",
          "2": "Shikamoo. Kushukuru.",
          "3": "Nyanya.",
          "4": "Ng'ombe."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 4,
        "total_pages": 4,
        "content": [
          "THE STRESS RULE — there is only one:",
          "the second-to-last syllable, always.",
          "",
          "sa-LA-ma    ra-FI-ki    ta-fa-DHA-li",
          "",
          "Hear the rhythm:                   Press 1",
          "   salama, rafiki, tafadhali",
          "",
          "Every syllable ends in a vowel, so",
          "words flow open, like Italian:",
          "ka-ri-bu, a-san-te, kwa-he-ri.",
          "",
          "That is the whole sound system. If you",
          "can say it, you can spell it — and if",
          "you can read it, you can say it."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Salama. Rafiki. Tafadhali."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "002",
    "trigger_word": "shikamoo",
    "trigger_card_number": 6,
    "title": "LESSON 2: GREETING IS A CEREMONY",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Greetings are not small talk here — they",
          "are the front door of the culture.",
          "",
          "To an elder, ALWAYS:",
          "",
          "   Shikamoo!                       Press 1",
          "   (I hold your feet — deep respect)",
          "",
          "The elder answers:",
          "",
          "   Marahaba.                       Press 2",
          "   (I accept — delighted)",
          "",
          "Address people well: bwana (sir),",
          "bibi (madam), mzee (elder). Age is",
          "honoured before anything else."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Shikamoo!",
          "2": "Marahaba."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Built on jambo (a matter, an affair):",
          "",
          "   Hujambo? — Sijambo!             Press 1",
          "   (Nothing wrong with you? —",
          "    Nothing wrong with me!)",
          "",
          "At any door, before entering, call:",
          "",
          "   Hodi! Hodi!                     Press 2",
          "",
          "And the answer that lets you in:",
          "",
          "   Karibu!                         Press 3",
          "   (Welcome — come close)",
          "",
          "No knocking needed. The voice knocks."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Hujambo? Sijambo!",
          "2": "Hodi! Hodi!",
          "3": "Karibu!"
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Words for life's moments:",
          "",
          "   pole — sympathy for any hardship",
          "   (said to the tired, the hurt,",
          "   the grieving — always kind)",
          "",
          "   hongera — congratulations!",
          "   heri — a blessing: kwa heri ya mwaka",
          "",
          "The courtesy triangle:",
          "",
          "   tafadhali — please",
          "   asante — thank you",
          "   samahani — excuse me",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
