  {
    "lesson_id": "003",
    "trigger_word": "mtoto",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 3: THE NOUN-CLASS ENGINE",
    "header_color": "yellow",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "English pluralizes at the END: child,",
          "children. Swahili changes the FRONT:",
          "",
          "   mtoto  ->  watoto                Press 1",
          "   (child)    (children)",
          "",
          "   mtu    ->  watu                  Press 2",
          "   (person)   (people)",
          "",
          "   mzazi  ->  wazazi                Press 3",
          "   (parent)   (parents)",
          "",
          "m- for one, wa- for many. This is the",
          "m/wa class — the PEOPLE class. Look at",
          "any card: the label under the word",
          "(m/wa) names its class."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Mtoto. Watoto.",
          "2": "Mtu. Watu.",
          "3": "Mzazi. Wazazi."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Not every family word plays this way:",
          "",
          "   ndugu -> ndugu   (siblings: no change)",
          "   dada  -> dada    (sisters: no change)",
          "   mama  -> mama    (mothers: no change)",
          "",
          "These live in the n/n class — the front",
          "stays still. Their card label says n/n.",
          "",
          "Every Swahili noun belongs to a class,",
          "and the class runs the grammar around",
          "it — you will see it echo through",
          "adjectives, numbers and 'of' later.",
          "",
          "Swahili's own name for a noun class is",
          "ngeli — you will meet it in the Notes.",
          "For now: read the label, learn the pair."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {}
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Three more classes wait in these cards:",
          "",
          "   u/n    ukoo   -> koo     (kinship)",
          "   ki/vi  kichwa -> vichwa  (things)",
          "   m/mi   mdomo  -> midomo  (body, trees)",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "u- names one, n- many — and the n- can",
          "hide: ukoo -> koo, uso -> nyuso.",
          "",
          "ki- names one, vi- many; before a vowel",
          "they glide to ch- and vy-.",
          "",
          "m/mi looks like m/wa, but only people",
          "take wa-. A thing that starts with m-",
          "takes mi-: mdomo -> midomo, not wadomo."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Ukoo. Koo. Kichwa. Vichwa. Mdomo. Midomo."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "004",
    "trigger_word": "wewe",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 4: I, YOU, AND UNSEEN 'IS'",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "The six people of every conversation:",
          "",
          "   mimi  I           sisi   we",
          "   wewe  you         ninyi  you all",
          "   yeye  he / she    wao    they",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "And 'is'? One word: ni. It never",
          "changes, for anyone:",
          "",
          "   Mimi ni mgeni.   I am a guest.",
          "   Wewe ni rafiki.  You are a friend.",
          "   Yeye ni jirani.  He is a neighbour.",
          "",
          "Hear the pattern:                  Press 2"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Mimi. Wewe. Yeye. Sisi. Ninyi. Wao.",
          "2": "Mimi ni mgeni. Wewe ni rafiki. Yeye ni jirani."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "The opposite is just as easy:",
          "",
          "   si = is not / am not / are not",
          "",
          "   Mimi si mzee.   I am not old.",
          "",
          "Questions come from little words:",
          "",
          "   nani?  who?      Wewe ni nani?",
          "   nini?  what?     Hii ni nini?",
          "   wapi?  where?",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "No word order gymnastics: say the",
          "sentence, place the question word."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Nani? Nini? Wapi? Wewe ni nani?"
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "005",
    "trigger_word": "moja",
