    "title": "LESSON 5: COUNTING FORMS",
    "header_color": "green",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Count to ten:",
          "",
          "   1 moja    2 mbili   3 tatu",
          "   4 nne     5 tano    6 sita",
          "   7 saba    8 nane    9 tisa",
          "  10 kumi",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "Two streams flow here: sita, saba,",
          "tisa and everything from ishirini (20)",
          "up came from Arabic. The rest is Bantu.",
          "Why care? Because only the Bantu",
          "numbers AGREE — the Arabic ones never",
          "change shape."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Moja. Mbili. Tatu. Nne. Tano. Sita. Saba. Nane. Tisa. Kumi."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Counting people, the class steps in:",
          "",
          "   watu wawili   two people",
          "   watu watatu   three people",
          "   watu sita     six people (Arabic:",
          "                 no agreement!)",
          "",
          "Hear the difference:               Press 1",
          "",
          "Yes — mbili becomes wawili: behind the",
          "counting form hides the stem -wili,",
          "and the class prefix rides in front.",
          "",
          "Big numbers stack simply:",
          "mia (100), elfu (1000),",
          "kumi na moja = eleven (ten and one)."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Watu wawili. Watu watatu. Watu sita."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "006",
    "trigger_word": "kuwa",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 6: THE VERB MACHINE",
    "header_color": "red",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Every verb on these cards starts with",
          "ku-. That is the 'to' of 'to eat':",
          "",
          "   kula     to eat",
          "   kunywa   to drink",
          "   kwenda   to go",
          "",
          "Strip the ku-, and what remains is the",
          "ROOT — the part that never changes:",
          "",
          "   kula -> -la    kupenda -> -penda",
          "",
          "Swahili verbs do not conjugate like",
          "European ones. They ASSEMBLE, like",
          "beads on a string. Next page: the",
          "string."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {}
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "WHO + WHEN + ROOT. That is the string.",
          "",
          "The WHO slot (people):",
          "   ni- I       tu- we",
          "   u-  you     m-  you all",
          "   a-  he/she  wa- they",
          "",
          "The WHEN slot, present tense: -na-",
          "",
          "   ni + na + penda = ninapenda",
          "   I am loving / I love",
          "",
          "   a + na + kwenda = anakwenda",
          "   she is going",
          "",
          "Hear the machine run:              Press 1"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Ninapenda. Anakwenda. Tunakula. Wanakunywa."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Swap the WHEN bead, swap the tense:",
          "",
          "   -na-  now       ninakula  I eat",
          "   -li-  past      nilikula  I ate",
          "   -ta-  future    nitakula  I will eat",
          "   -me-  done      nimekula  I have eaten",
          "",
          "Hear one verb, four times:         Press 1",
          "",
          "One quirk: tiny roots keep their ku-.",
          "kula and kunywa hold it in the present:",
          "ninakula, not 'ninala'. The rhythm",
          "needs the extra syllable.",
          "",
          "The machine never breaks. Trust it."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Ninakula. Nilikula. Nitakula. Nimekula."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "007",
    "trigger_word": "chakula",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 7: KI/VI, JI/MA, AND N",
    "header_color": "yellow",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "The food bowl serves three new noun",
          "families at once.",
          "",
          "KI/VI — ki- for one, vi- for many:",
          "",
          "   kiazi  ->  viazi      potatoes",
          "",
          "Before a vowel, ki- glides to ch-,",
          "and vi- to vy-:",
          "",
          "   chakula -> vyakula    foods",
          "",
          "JI/MA — the plural simply puts ma- on:",
          "",
          "   yai     -> mayai      eggs",
          "   chungwa -> machungwa  oranges",
          "   embe    -> maembe     mangoes"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {}
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "JI/MA marks fruit, eggs, and many",
          "everyday things: plural puts ma- on.",
          "",
          "And the N class? It does nothing at",
          "all — and that is its charm:",
          "",
          "   nyama -> nyama     meat is meat",
          "   chai  -> chai      tea is tea",
          "",
          "Some N-class nouns only exist plural:",
          "maji (water) is grammatically 'they'.",
          "",
          "Hear the families:                 Press 1",
          "",
          "Four families down: m/wa, ki/vi,",
          "ji/ma, n/n. The card label always",
          "tells you which one you are holding."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Kiazi. Viazi. Chakula. Vyakula. Yai. Mayai. Nyama. Maji."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "And one more family, right in the bowl:",
          "",
          "   u/u — the class with no plural at all.",
          "",
          "   ugali    stiff maize porridge",
          "   unga     flour",
          "   uji      thin porridge",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "One spoonful or a whole pot, the word",
          "never moves. Compare u/n from Lesson 3:",
          "ukoo -> koo has a plural; u/u has none.",
          "",
          "u- also opens the words for qualities —",
          "ukweli (truth), uhuru (freedom) — things",
          "nobody counts one by one."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Ugali. Unga. Uji."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
